S2-F3 is **CONFIRMED**.

**The claim holds.** `measure.py:863-865` puts `result.runtime_version = handle.version` inside `if result.cold_load_s is None`, two levels deep, while `request_seed` immediately above it is stamped on every visit. `grep -n "handle.version" ohyesmlx/*.py` returns exactly one hit — that line. And the real handle *does* carry a fresh build each launch: `Runtime.start` calls `self.version()` at `runtimes.py:2327` and passes it to the `Handle` at `:2355`, so the loop is the only place the later build is dropped.

**Reproduction** (`tests/test_fable_s2_f3.py`, both failing): a runtime reporting `0.25.6` then `0.25.18` across the two visits a 9-batch budget forces, yields

- `PASS` stamped `'0.25.6'` on a row holding all nine samples from two builds
- a `results.jsonl` line with `status: PASS, runtime_version: "0.25.6"` — and I ran both version-reading guards over that record: `_check_known_runtime_versions` only refuses `unknown` prefixes and guard 4 sees one string, so both accept it

A one-build control (`0.25.6` twice) stays `PASS`, so the build change is the discriminating variable. Applying the finding's fix out-of-process turns the test green, so it is falsifiable.

**One correction to the remedy.** The fix direction as stated isn't sufficient by itself: `run_cells` calls `_set_status` after every `measured` visit (`measure.py:663`), which rewrites status and reason from the samples. My first simulated fix set FAIL and returned `"measured"` — the row came back `PASS`. It has to return `"skip"` (the shape `stream_experts_missing` already uses) or record the mismatch where `_set_status` can't overwrite it.

**Not verified:** no real Osaurus update mid-run (no server/engine/app started); whether an update can land inside the ~30 s cooldown is a timing question I didn't test; only the fake runtime was exercised.

